Z||000003||Seanh||08-29-2000||02:26 PM||||Well, I have never driven a North* in a Fiero, so I dont think that I would really need to turbo or super it, but Its just the thought, ya know. I am sick of others at school telling me what a wast of time this car is, and how its such a peice of crap, and always wanting to race me with these new Cameros and stuff. I cant wait to see the look on there face when my peice of crap just blows there doors off. You think I should just stick with the North* and not turbo or super it? Im getting that impression from other I have talked to. They tell me that this new engine is more then enough power for me. My existing Turbo system I recently found out it would not work on this new engine, like the guy said the first time he looked at it. So they are being sold to help pay for the new engine. And there is already tons of people on a list to buy this setup, but Im pretty sure it already sold, when he comes up with the money. Perhaps I should just stick with a new engine and put off the Turbo or Super chargers till later.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||08-29-2000||02:37 PM||||Right...  Do the Northstar swap first and get the car in running condition ie. doesn't overheat.  Adding a turbo or sc would be cool but overkill.  You're going to have a weight to horsepower ratio that's going to be big, it should be plenty quick!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||BBBAD GT||08-29-2000||03:47 PM||||Don't take this the wrong way, but why do you want to upgrade to the N* if your current combo does high 12's now (I think thats what you said in an earlier post)?  <P>IMO, if you put a bone stock N* in the car now, it 'might' just keep up with your old combo for a lot more dollars spent.  Last time I checked, the late model Camaros/TA's are only doing low 13's and maybe an occasional 12 with a really good driver bone stock.<P>If you want speed, and unlimited speed at that, do the SBC IMO.      ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||08-29-2000||04:28 PM||||Thats right, about high 12's. Like I said in the past, the previous owner didnt take care of this car. The engine is starting to burn oil and smoke, so I kinda decided to put in a North* in instead of a V6. I have heard that North* is the most advanced engine on the market, and what good gas milage it gets. I get about 10-12 mpg now, and it has room for improvment. Will the SBC IMO do better then high 12's? I never looked into this engine yet.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||08-29-2000||05:06 PM||||A few years back Gary Zumalt had an ad showing a supercharged Northstar mounted in a Fiero cradle. I think he is the guy you'd want to talk to. 816-966-0595. Be prepared to drop a few extra thousand dollars for the SC and ECM mods.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
Z||000008||88formula||08-29-2000||05:19 PM||||I think Design One is designing a twin turbo setup for the Norhstar Fiero. <A HREF="http://www.designonesystems.com/northstar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.designonesystems.com/northstar/index.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||rprevo||08-29-2000||06:12 PM|||| I have a question?<BR>Meaning no disrespect.<BR>I have done a lot of work on cars when the<BR>check engine light comes on and the ECM<BR>code says one thing and the problem<BR>is another and you spend countless hrs<BR>and dollars getting to the root cause.<BR>what is the real advantage to fuel inj.<BR>and a ECM  ?<BR> Old fasioned Small block owner.<P>  Ray<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000010||terryk||08-29-2000||06:19 PM||||I rarely see a situation where I can't relate the failure to the malf code. I admit that it takes a certainly level of understanding of the system to figure it out. <P>There is no way GM could make malfs so specific that you get "Malf 77 - TPS Bottom Screw Loose"<P>The real benifit? Tons better control of the engine in all situations (except some of the early CCC ECM's).<P>IMHO<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000011||EdsB52||08-29-2000||06:54 PM||||Advantage to fuel injection? That's a no-brainer. You get timed pulses of fuel that are perfectly atomized spraying into the intake valve. some of the biggest restrictions or delimas with an intake manifold / carburetor are the turns it must take, the loss of flow due to diameter restrictions and rough surface of intake wall, and loss of atomization between the carburetor and intake valve (cylinder). With port fuel injection you avoid all of that crap. If you have fuel injection (port or TB) you need a way to control it, hence, the ECM. Port fuel injection with a supercharger or blower is the ultimate dream.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||08-29-2000||07:04 PM||||After spending the better part of 2 days trying to get a carb/points dist. powered truck to run, I must tell you that I had much rather swap out sensors than set float levels, point gap, dwell angles, idle speed, power valves, metering rods, muffler bearings. . . ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||08-29-2000||07:49 PM||||I think the biggest problem is those muffler bearings.. they're just a pain to adjust.. and don't even get me started about the johnson rod's!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||10
Z||000014||Will||08-29-2000||09:44 PM||||A northstar fiero will be so powerful you won't know what to do with it.<BR>     I am working on a N* conversion, and have been entertaining ideas about how to use a pair of T-bird turbo-coupe turbos or something similar (I want water cooled turbos!!!!!!!). A supercharger I think would be much harder.<BR>     I also have some ideas for intercooling.<BR>     Are you doing the conversion yourself?<P>Going from a 2.8 to an N* will be a huge step. For safety's sake you should drive the N/A N* for a while and get used to it before you seriously consider adding FI.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||08-29-2000||10:46 PM||||I am still deciding weather I should do it myself or not. I am problly thinking that I will be doing it. Iv changed other engines, but not a Fiero. We have a 94 Caddy with the N* engine that I drive alot, so Im pretty used to what it can deliver. I cant wait to feel that power in a Fiero. I am going to put in the engine and then Ill drive it for a while (untill I get the money to Turbo it) and see if I really think Ill need it. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000016||stimpy||08-29-2000||11:36 PM||||Ok, let's review one more time- you have a twin turbo set up on a tired 2.8 that runs high 12's. Instead of puting that set up on a fresh 60 degree V6, you want to sh!tcan it and start over with a Northstar swap, then add forced induction. All so you can beat some Camaro's? You must have some very Bad-assed Camaro's in your neck of the woods. <P>I'm still feeling kinda Missouri about your twin turbos (ya know, the old "Show-Me State"). I can't help feeling that if you got the scratch to install a supercharged Northstar, ya oughta be able to come up with a camera and scanner time at Kinko's. I recall reading in a post you saying that you are not very mechanically inclined, yet you've previously done swaps?  You buy a car from someone who made a considerable investment in the twin turbo set up, but then neglected it?<BR>I'm so close to calling bullshit that I can barely contain myself. <P>Hey, if I'm wrong, then my bad. Athough you obviously have plenty of money to console yourself with. But if you are making it up, ha ha, jokes over, time to come clean...  ||||reg||4
Z||000017||Archie||08-30-2000||12:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>I think Design One is designing a twin turbo setup for the Norhstar Fiero. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>At the ElPaso show I talked to the guy (A Pontiac dealer out of Bloomington) whos car has been at Kevin's for more than a year. His car is supposed to be the one that Kevin is prototyping the Twin Turbo NStar for. He told me that Kevin has put the project asside for now due to lack of funds and time. The customer will just be getting a plain NStar for now, he expects to get his car back sometime next year.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||rprevo||08-30-2000||07:53 AM|||| You guy's said a whole lot of impressive<BR>words about how great an ecm is but how<BR>many of you guys really think when that<BR>car decides to die for no apparent reason<BR>on the side of the road your really going<BR>to be able to fix it. And as far as points<BR>go You don't need a ECM to have an HEI distributer and how many times have you<BR>ever found a Faulty wire in the Harness<BR>and spent the money on the sensor. <BR>Any one that says this stuff is a no-brainer<BR>I could set a fault in your harness that<BR>could drive you crazy.<P> Ray||||reg||6
Z||000019||BBBAD GT||08-30-2000||09:31 AM||||Seanh:  IMO what I'd do is either rebuild the 2.8 up to 3.1 with good stuff or just swap in a low mileage 3.4 (best value) with the Twins and <B>properly tuned</B> (10-12mpg is not right for a 2.8 V6, unless you are on boost 100% of the time).  You will save yourself alot of heartache, and most likely tons of cash for the same amount of speed.  <P>You quoted earlier <B>"I am sick of others at school telling me what a wast of time this car is"</B>  Are you in highschool/college?  If you are in school, then I got to hand it to you that you can tackle a "not normal" engine swap in your backyard without a regular job.  Well, the swap hasn't happen yet, so I guess we'll find out........<P> <p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Dan86gt3.8||08-30-2000||09:55 AM||||If you are worried about beating camaros put a 125-150 shot on the n* then see who can keep up.........  If you couldn't keep up n/a a win would just be a squeze of a button away.  someone already has this setup, he was in kit car a few years back his car is painted like a commet, I just rember it was a sweet ride<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>15" konig monsoon's<BR>rear swaybar/ and urethane on rear.<BR>Soon to come urethane all the way around. and new yellow paint w/ raised spoiler!!!!<BR>(also in way future years forced induction on my 3.8!!!!!!!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000021||HYPTNOTiSE||08-30-2000||11:11 AM||||I want to see twin turbo pics.  Are you running a manual or automatic? Where are the turbo's mounted? What kind of turbos are they? Are you intercooling?  Are the turbos sequential?  How much boost are you running?  What type fuel? How big are your injectors? What's your compression ratio?  What do you have for engine management?  A twin turbo setup is easily camable of 300hp at the crank.  Steve Hamm put 322 at the crank with one turbo and only 10psi of boost. And on a 2.8 at that.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||N_Man||08-30-2000||01:21 PM||||yup if it was me id keep the turbo setup with a fresh V6.  Ford 2.3 turbos are very capable of 12's or 13's with a little bit of work.  So i dont see why maybee a 3.4 twin turbo in a lighter car couldnt pull maybee an 11 if properly tuned.||||reg||1
Z||000023||BBBAD GT||08-30-2000||01:58 PM||||HYPTNOTiSE:<P>You forgot to say "and I'll race you when your done"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Seanh||08-30-2000||03:37 PM||||Ok Wow, tons of question. Ill do my best. In the order I remember. As far as the beating the Cameros remark. There is one Camero here that has a supercharged 451 chevy with Nitros. He really does blow my doors off. I just put a 12 speaker stero system in my Fiero, so the money for the North* wont be here for a while... Im just looking into it now... Ok as for HYPTNOTiSE. I am running a 4spd Auto. Turbos are mounted in the trunk with a little modifycation. They are Turbo-Dynes. not Intercooling. they are sequential. I usally only run about 50% boost, unless im racing. Most of the time I use 98 octane gas. The injectors are 16 gph? does that sound right? compression ratio, not sure off the top of head. what exacly do you mean by engine management? I forget who said this, but Im a Senior in HS. And people, dont get all crazy on me just because I brought up the North* topic. Im am not ready to buy yet, im just getting an idea. I just might rebuild the V6 or go up to a 3.1. I also think that if I went with a new tranny like a 5 spd or 6 spd, I could also do a little better.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000025||HYPTNOTiSE||08-30-2000||06:14 PM||||God i wanna see pics.COME ON PICS! PICS! PICS! PICS!  What do you do about heat in the trunk compartment?  Injectors are ususally rated in pounds per hour, not gallons.  Engine managment i mean what type computer are you using. Surely it's not stock. Which 4 speed auto are you running?  Man 98 octance gas must get expensive.  What size is your small turbo and what size is your larger turbo?  How many pounds of boost do you run? How do you solve the problem with opening waste gates and such? What type of fuel system to do you have? What is internally done to the motor?  <BR>Oh and BBBADGT...If his car runs like steve hamms, i dont want to race, i'd go home with my head down and my tail between my legs.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||stimpy||08-30-2000||08:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Will the SBC IMO do better then high 12's? I never looked into this engine yet.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually I think the SBC IMO would be the perfect engine for your application. At 12,000 rpm it puts out 739 hp, yet it generates 720 ft lbs torque at a mere 3000 rpm. This is due in part to the high strength unobtainium forged rods and pistons. They are very light, weighing in around 27 pounds. (32 if you get the optional, but oh so essential solid gold valve covers) They are easily adapted to forced induction, with advanced engine management system that can be controlled through your CD player. Just turn the volume up for more boost! I looked on the IMO website, and if you order now, they will send you a specially designed racing hat, complete with aerodynamic rear facing bill. You'll be the coolest kid at the Carl's Jr.! <P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||HYPTNOTiSE||08-30-2000||08:42 PM||||I mean why install a supercharger on your northstar. Shaun Hammitt just finished his install and now has 3 northstar engines powering his ride.  He had to remove two of his speakers from the inside, the ones frenched into the fender stayed, to make room for the 3rd northstar.  He removed the passenger seat and just uses the amp from the permium sound system as that northstars computer.  Works great apparently.  He's using the stock automatic transmission for all three engines, all he needed was a simple adapter plate.  his car now runs low 7's in the 1/4 mile without any roll cage of any sort. That's just added weight.  He almost ran out of money doing the project cos he needed a few hose clamps and stuff, and didn't feel like going to the bank. HE just took his all aluminum 60 degree block to the salvage yard and they gave him 36 cents per pound.  He also had to skimp on the muffler bearings, had to go bronze instead of titanium.  I hear muffler bearings from a 69 pinto will work also, is this true?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000028||Sillhouette.com||08-30-2000||08:52 PM||||Well fist of all I must flush out some liars.  If your car does low 12s with a twin turbo setup than Racetechs turbocarger should not be doing low 12s as well.  One of you is not on the level.  Anyways as far as a twin turbo setup is concerned I don't think the high heat spiderweb lookalike ready to spring a leak turbocharger is viable in a rear engine mounted car unless you do some serious cooling designs.  Add to all this the fact that a shoe horn is required to install a Northstar in the back of a fiero will make the ventilation a big problem.  I have played around with a Supercharger on an Aurora with the 4.0L Northstar not the 4.6 that's in the cadillac.  Same engine just a bit different displacement.  The install was very easy because the waterpump is run off the fwd cam on the other side of your standard belt also the new computer system was able to adjust for boost quite easy.  Never had it out for a run just hooked it up and started the car.  It idled fine and no heat buildup was felt throughout the pressurized pipes.  In short I think supercharging is a way more reliable cost effective setup than turbocharging.  <P>Hope this helps  Later||||reg||1
Z||000029||Seanh||08-30-2000||11:11 PM||||Just to be correct, mine did high 12's about 12.8, and recently as high as 13.6 <BR>Ill answer the rest of the questions tomarrow.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
